# Gea template conventions

Follow Gea’s “just JavaScript” model: class fields for local UI state, stores for shared state, getters for derived values, and shared object/array props that children mutate in place. Prefer function components for presentational leaves.

## Props: mutate shared objects/arrays (no callback wiring)

Objects and arrays passed as props are the parent’s reactive proxy. Children should mutate them directly (`push` / `splice`) instead of lifting state with `checked` + `onChange` callbacks.

```tsx
// BAD — React-style callback wiring for a shared selection
<FilterCheckbox checked={selected.includes(city)} onChange={() => toggle(city)} />

// GOOD — pass the reactive array; child mutates it
<FilterCheckbox label={city} selected={this.selectedCities} />
```

## Class fields, not nested `state = {}`

```tsx
// BAD
state = { searchQuery: '', selected: new Set() }

// GOOD
searchQuery = ''
selected: string[] = []
```

Prefer reactive `string[]` over `Set` so Gea’s array mutation tracking applies.

## Function vs class components

Gea’s compiler picks the call style from the **parent**:

- Reactive class parents (e.g. `SiteHeader`) mount mapped children with `new Child()` → child must be a **class** (`SectionNavLink`, `PortalNavLink`, `TeamMemberCard`)
- Static class parents (e.g. `MeetingList`) often call children as `Child(props, d)` → child must be a **function** (`MeetingListItem`, `MeetingAssetLink`, `FilterCheckbox`)
- Function parents (e.g. `TeamGroupSection`) use `mount(Child, …)` → child can be a class

Prefer mapping portal/nav leaves **directly in `SiteHeader`** (same pattern as `SectionNavLink`) rather than nesting a second keyed list inside a static wrapper.

Also use a **class** for local UI state or lifecycle (`onAfterRender` / `dispose`).

Mismatch symptoms: `Class constructor X cannot be invoked without 'new'`, or `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'child')`.

## Always pass `key` in `.map()`

```tsx
{items.map((item) => (
  <ItemCard key={item.id} item={item} />
))}
```

Mapping to a child component (with a single root) is the safe pattern for nested markup. Prefer that over unrolling fixed lists when the data already lives in an array.

## Avoid fragile template locals

Prefer getters / `this.props` / template parameter destructuring over `const` locals that capture external data inside `template()`:

```tsx
// RISKY — some compiler paths drop template-local bindings
template() {
  const [first] = about.partners
  return <a href={first.href}>{first.name}</a>
}

// GOOD — reference module data or getters directly
template() {
  return <a href={about.partners[0].href}>{about.partners[0].name}</a>
}
```

Derive prep arrays **outside** the class when needed (`Object.entries(…).map(…)`).

## Avoid mixed static text + `{expr}` in one text node

```tsx
// BAD — city may render blank
<span> — {site.city}</span>

// GOOD — expression alone; put the dash in CSS or a separate node
<span class="sites-list__city">{site.city}</span>
```

## Prefer no fragments as `.map()` item roots

`<>...</>` inside `.map()` has triggered `elN is not defined` failures. Use a single parent element per item.
